{"id":55,"course_id":14,"title":"Progressive Delivery and Rollback","summary":"Release with canaries, blue/green, feature flags, and measured rollback.","sequence":3,"body":"## Mental model\nProgressive delivery limits blast radius by exposing changes gradually and watching real signals. Rollback must be rehearsed before the incident.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice defining canary metrics, pause conditions, automated rollback, and ArgoCD sync behavior.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nWrite a canary decision rule using latency, error rate, saturation, and business signal.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-cicd-release-engineering","course_category":"CI/CD","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":271,"term":"CI/CD and Release Engineering","context":"CI/CD","definition":"Build safe pipelines for container builds, tests, image signing, environment promotion, progressive delivery, and rollback."},{"id":272,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":273,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":274,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":275,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":163,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Progressive Delivery and Rollback?","answer":"Release with canaries, blue/green, feature flags, and measured rollback.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":164,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":165,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
